course: dictionary
lesson: 2148
title: A little wood called a copse
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A copse is a small group of trees growing close together. It is a little wood, not a big forest. Look at the words around it to check: «At last he suggests a rest on some tree-stumps by a copse (a small wood)». The brackets tell you the meaning straight away.
explain: Use the place to guess too. A copse is near trees, fields and water, so you might find one by a river or in the countryside. In «The Wind in the Willows», the river bank and fields are dotted with copses, which means they are scattered here and there.
explain: Test your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Try "a rest by a little wood" instead of "a rest by a copse". If it still makes sense, your meaning is right. If it doesn't, check again.
fact: A copse is a small group of trees growing close together, so it is a little wood.
fact: Some people call a copse a coppice. Here, a coppice means a small wood where trees are cut back regularly so they grow again.
fact: Animals such as rabbits, foxes and pheasants shelter in a copse, where they can hide and feel safe.
fact: Copses are common in British and Irish nature lessons, maps and stories.
fact: The countryside in «The Wind in the Willows» is dotted with copses.
exercise: «At last he suggests a rest on some tree-stumps by a copse.» What is a copse? | A steep road up a hill | *A small group of trees growing close together | A stream full of fish | A wide, open river bank
exercise: Rabbits, foxes and pheasants shelter in a copse. Why might they go there? | To watch the sunset from a hill | To sell food at a market | To swim in the river | *To hide and feel safe among the trees
exercise: A copse can also be called a coppice. What is a coppice? | A large field of wheat | A rocky cliff by the sea | A kind of boat used on rivers | *A small wood where trees are cut back so they grow again
test: Rabbits and pheasants hide in a copse. Where are they hiding? | On the top of a tall tower | In the sea, under the waves | In a busy shop | *In a small wood of trees
test: You meet the word copse and are not sure what it means. What is a good way to check? | Count the letters and guess | Skip the word and never think about it again | *Swap in "a little wood" and see if the sentence still makes sense | Decide it must be a type of animal
exam: In «The Wind in the Willows», the river bank and fields are dotted with copses. What does dotted mean here? | Flooded with water | Covered in deep snow | *Scattered here and there | Painted in dots of colour
